78 #edb/ref 17592186431290 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431342 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "4a97b187-3262-5c06-b7ed-fed9c7d6dc5c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431298 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431312 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186431313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186431285 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431345 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a6feb552-f724-5d46-86df-32005b0ffcc7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431349 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431349 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126939" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431349 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431349 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/b1/b1a063fd261e790a8c3a56f741d0356f963300c3fbd88ddde03aa1744ef81053.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431349 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "420adaa9-fbd3-5686-bca6-a55b93dae680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431349 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{4}$ and $Q(x,y)=({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3}).$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-4{y}^{3},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}({x}^{2}-{y}^{4})\\,dx+({x}^{3}y-x{y}^{3})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}-(-4{y}^{3})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}y+3{y}^{3}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 4,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}3y({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3\\cdot r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot ((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\cdot {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{4}3{r}^{4}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,{|}_{0}^{2\\pi }\\cdot \\frac{3{r}^{5}}{5}\\,{|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\pi )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =(1-1)\\cdot (\\frac{3(4)^{5}}{5}-\\frac{3(1)^{5}}{5}) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431351 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431345 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431351 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126855" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431351 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431351 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/f2/f26ee97bb47430c6a5ed314af007f49415295f682eeb474af33217ef58999758.png)\n\nSuppose that $F$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}$ defined over the region $D$ above. Given that \n\n$$\n{∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=4,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=9,\\,{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r=-5,\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}$ is oriented counterclockwise and ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ are oriented clockwise, calculate the value of ${∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431351 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5c72e07b-dd70-5ff7-8f10-0939e87dedc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431351 #edb/ref 1074 "To calculate the total circulation of a vector field over the boundary of a region containing holes, we add together the circulations over each *positively oriented* boundary.\n\nRemember that:\n\n- the positive direction is *counterclockwise* for the *outer* curve ${C}_{1}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{1}$ counterclockwise), and\n\n- the positive direction is *clockwise* for the *inner* curves ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ (because the region $D$ is always on the left as we traverse ${C}_{2}$ and ${C}_{3}$ clockwise).\n\nThe diagram below shows the positive orientations of all three curves.\n\n![](images/7b/7b68ab924946d0509b1666b5c0795a7f8987c32aef88ca3ab72746663e9428a8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}F\\cdot \\text{d}r & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r+{∮}_{{C}_{3}}F\\cdot \\text{d}r \\\\  & =4+9+(-5) \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431353 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186431341 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431353 #edb/ref 1069 "q-126938" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431353 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431353 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/5c/5ca9f071eaeebb0b31fba99a48ca8f08bd6b4b73588838aad7b5f513c283fd6c.png)\n\nCalculate ${∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy,$ where $D$ is the annular region shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431353 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "93158d64-29fd-580d-9bf5-f56c5e2adb43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186431353 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D,$ then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n{∮}_{C}P\\,dx+Q\\,dy={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nGreen's theorem can be extended to include regions containing holes. In such cases, we can apply Green's theorem in the same way as before.\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)=-{x}^{2}y-{y}^{3}$ and $Q(x,y)={x}^{3}+x{y}^{2}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2},\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}-({x}^{2}y+{y}^{3})\\,dx+({x}^{3}+x{y}^{2})\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}3{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-(-{x}^{2}-3{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4{x}^{2}+4{y}^{2}\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ can be represented in polar coordinates as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,1\\le r\\le 3,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le 2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nRecall that the change of variables formula for polar coordinates gives\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, our double integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}4({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =4{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta {\\int }_{1}^{3}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi {\\int }_{1}^{2}{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r \\\\  & =8\\pi \\cdot {\\frac{1}{4}{r}^{4}|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =2\\pi \\cdot ({3}^{4}-{1}^{4}) \\\\  & =160\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186441619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186441620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186441621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186441622 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186441623 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending Green's Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186441620 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050560 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441589 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "12c751f0-6856-54db-a651-f054b3719043" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441590 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{89}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441590 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441590 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "12bc592f-463c-5b10-94de-5b44d176d00d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441591 #edb/ref 1080 "-\\frac{1}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441591 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441591 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1e63af1d-9e8f-51ce-b89b-69668a98103f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441592 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\pi " #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441592 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186441592 #